+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report for `uom`
+
+Property-based tests were added with `hegeltest` v0.28.2 (library crate name `hegel`),
+following the hegel skill workflow. The crate already has an extensive quickcheck suite
+(macro-generated across all storage types); that suite was left untouched and used as
+*evidence* for properties. The hegel tests were added to the existing test files next to the
+quickcheck tests they generalize, using `f64` storage (broadest generators, NaN/infinity
+included unless noted).
+
+## Properties tested
+
+All tests live in existing test files, gated on `all(feature = "f64", feature = "std")`
+(matching how neighbouring tests are gated).
+
+| # | Property | Test | Evidence |
+|---|----------|------|----------|
+| 1 | `new::<kilometer>(v).get::<kilometer>()` round-trips (same non-base unit in/out) | `tests::quantity::hegel_props::hegel_new_get_same_unit_roundtrip` (`src/tests/quantity.rs`) | `get` unit test in the same file; `from_base`/`change_base` quickcheck tests in `src/tests/system.rs`. The `assume` guard (`v * km / km ≈ v`) mirrors the self-consistency discard in the existing `change_base` quickcheck test. |
+| 2 | `new::<kilometer>(v).get::<meter>() ≈ v * coefficient` (conversion scales by the unit coefficient) | `tests::quantity::hegel_props::hegel_get_base_unit_scales_by_coefficient` (`src/tests/quantity.rs`) | `new`/`get` unit tests; `to_base` quickcheck test in `src/tests/system.rs`. |
+| 3 | `FromStr` never panics on arbitrary input (garbage text plus quantity-shaped strings) | `tests::quantity::hegel_props::hegel_from_str_never_panics` (`src/tests/quantity.rs`) | `FromStr` impl in `src/quantity.rs` documents three error variants; `from_str` unit test exercises them; parse-robustness pattern from the property catalogue. |
+| 4 | Format via `into_format_args` (both `DisplayStyle`s) then parse recovers the quantity exactly | `tests::quantity::hegel_props::hegel_display_parse_roundtrip` (`src/tests/quantity.rs`) | Evolved from the existing `round_trip` unit test in the same file, which only checks the value `1.0` and only `Abbreviation` style. |
+| 5 | `FromStr` accepts all three documented unit-name forms (abbreviation, singular, plural) for `meter` and `kilometer` | `tests::quantity::hegel_props::hegel_from_str_accepts_all_unit_name_forms` (`src/tests/quantity.rs`) | `FromStr` in `src/quantity.rs` matches `$abbreviation \| $singular \| $plural`; `from_str` unit test checks a few concrete instances. |
+| 6 | Addition homomorphism: `Length(a) + Length(b) == Length(a + b)` exactly | `tests::system::hegel_props::hegel_add_homomorphism` (`src/tests/system.rs`) | Existing `add` quickcheck test in the same file. |
+| 7 | Affine unit round-trip: `new::<degree_fahrenheit>(v).get::<degree_fahrenheit>()` returns `v` within `16·ε·(\|v\| + 459.67)`; NaN/infinities round-trip exactly | `tests::system::hegel_props::hegel_affine_unit_roundtrip` (`src/tests/system.rs`) | `from_base`/`to_base` quickcheck tests exercise each direction of the kelvin↔fahrenheit conversion; this composes them. Tolerance derived from the implementation (`to_base`: `(v + c) * k`; `from_base`: `v / k - c` in `src/system.rs`). See "Unsound properties abandoned" for the first, stricter version. |
+| 8 | Stateful model: a `Length` in base units behaves exactly like a plain `f64` under arbitrary interleavings of `+=`, `-=`, `*=` (scalar), `/=` (scalar, zero included), `%=`, and negation | `tests::system::hegel_props::hegel_length_behaves_like_f64_model` (`src/tests/system.rs`), state machine `LengthModel` | Existing `add_assign`/`sub_assign`/`mul_assign`/`div_assign`/`rem_assign` quickcheck tests (`non_big` module) and `neg` (`signed` module) each check a single operation; the model test checks sequences. |
+| 9 | `Quantity::atan2` agrees exactly with `f64::atan2` on the underlying values (full range incl. NaN/inf) | `si::angle::tests::hegel_props::hegel_atan2_matches_underlying_float` (`src/si/angle.rs`) | Direct hegel port of the `atan2` quickcheck test in the same file. |
+| 10 | `Angle::sin_cos()` is consistent with calling `Angle::sin()` and `Angle::cos()` separately | `si::angle::tests::hegel_props::hegel_sin_cos_consistent_with_sin_and_cos` (`src/si/angle.rs`) | `sin_cos` doc comment ("Computes the value of both the sine and cosine of the angle") and its delegation to `Float::sin_cos` in `src/si/angle.rs`. |
+
+The quickcheck dev-dependency was kept: the existing suite covers many storage types
+(f32, big/rational/complex under non-default features) that were not ported.
+
+## Test results
+
+`cargo test` (default features): **all pass**.
+
+```
+test result: ok. 710 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out   (lib tests)
+test result: ok. 272 passed; 0 failed; 1 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out   (doc tests)
+```
+
+All 10 hegel tests pass:
+
+- `si::angle::tests::hegel_props::hegel_atan2_matches_underlying_float` — pass
+- `si::angle::tests::hegel_props::hegel_sin_cos_consistent_with_sin_and_cos` — pass
+- `tests::quantity::hegel_props::hegel_new_get_same_unit_roundtrip` — pass
+- `tests::quantity::hegel_props::hegel_get_base_unit_scales_by_coefficient` — pass
+- `tests::quantity::hegel_props::hegel_from_str_never_panics` — pass
+- `tests::quantity::hegel_props::hegel_display_parse_roundtrip` — pass
+- `tests::quantity::hegel_props::hegel_from_str_accepts_all_unit_name_forms` — pass
+- `tests::system::hegel_props::hegel_add_homomorphism` — pass
+- `tests::system::hegel_props::hegel_affine_unit_roundtrip` — pass (after reformulating the tolerance; see below)
+- `tests::system::hegel_props::hegel_length_behaves_like_f64_model` — pass
+
+Sanity checks that the tests actually generate and shrink: (a) the first version of the
+affine round-trip property failed and hegel shrank it to the minimal counterexample
+`v = 0.0`; (b) a deliberately injected bug in the model test's `neg` rule (mutating the
+subject but not the model) was caught and shrunk to a minimal 2-step sequence
+(`add_assign(1.0)`, `neg`).
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+**None.** The one failure encountered was investigated and judged to be an unsound property
+(floating-point behaviour the library never promised), not a library bug — details below.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+**Affine round-trip with ulps-relative tolerance** (first version of property 7). Asserted
+`Test::assert_approx_eq(&v, &t.get::<degree_fahrenheit>())`, i.e. equality within
+`0.5·ε` absolute + 3 ulps relative. Hegel shrank the failure to the minimal counterexample:
+
+```
+let v = 0.0;
+left  = 0.0
+right = -5.684341886080802e-14
+```
+
+Investigation: `to_base` computes `(v + 459.67) * (5/9)` and `from_base` computes
+`k / (5/9) - 459.67` (`src/system.rs`, `from_base`/`to_base`). Each rounding step
+contributes error relative to `|v + c|`, not `|v|`, so near `v = 0` the round-trip is off
+by ~`459.67 · ε ≈ 5.7e-14` — inherent floating-point roundoff of an affine conversion, not
+a wrong formula. Corroborating evidence that the maintainers do not promise this: the
+fahrenheit→fahrenheit round-trip cases inside the `from_base`/`to_base` quickcheck tests in
+`src/tests/system.rs` are commented out, and `src/quantity.rs` documents that units with a
+non-zero constant are not supported as base units. The property was reformulated with the
+mathematically justified absolute bound `16·ε·(|v| + |c|)` (still falsifiable — a wrong
+conversion formula is off by many orders of magnitude more) rather than dropped entirely.
+
+No other property was abandoned.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+Overall the skill worked very well — every hegel test compiled on the first attempt and the
+whole flow (setup, generators, state machine, shrinking) behaved exactly as documented.
+Specific notes:
+
+1. **MSRV guidance was accurate and necessary.** `cargo add --dev hegeltest` failed with
+   `error: no version of crate 'hegeltest' can maintain uom's rust-version of 1.68.0`.
+   The reference's Setup section ("retry with `--ignore-rust-version`") resolved it
+   immediately. Without that note this would have been a plausible abandon-hegel moment.
+
+2. **Shrinking quality matched the pitch.** The affine round-trip failure shrank to `v = 0.0`
+   and the injected model-test bug shrank to a 2-step sequence — both genuinely minimal.
+   The `Step N: rule_name` / `let draw_1 = ...` replay output for state machines is very
+   readable, but it is not described in the reference's Stateful Testing section; a
+   one-line example of failure output would help agents interpret it.
+
+3. **Gotcha 10 ("passing tests print nothing") is easy to under-act on.** I verified
+   generation by mutation-testing the state machine (temporarily breaking a rule). The skill
+   could explicitly recommend the mutation check for *model tests* in particular, where a
+   vacuous test (e.g. rules that never fire due to `assume`) is silent.
+
+4. **`#[hegel::state_machine]` worked as documented**, including `tc.draw` inside rules and
+   the invariant being checked after every rule. The reference's note that `run` consumes
+   the machine (assertions must live in an invariant) was useful and correct.
+
+5. **Minor gap: no guidance on macro-heavy/generic test suites.** uom's existing quickcheck
+   tests are generated inside a `storage_types!` macro over ~20 storage types, and the
+   porting checklist ("ported coverage must be a superset of the original") is not
+   realistically achievable within a bounded task for such suites — hegel tests here were
+   written for concrete `f64` storage alongside the retained quickcheck suite. porting.md
+   item 2 covers *cfg-gated* suites; a note on *type-generic macro* suites (write hegel
+   tests for representative concrete types, keep the old suite) would have saved
+   deliberation.
+
+6. **Minor gap: interaction of `#[hegel::test]` with crate-level lints.** uom enables
+   `#![warn(unused_results, trivial_casts, ...)]`; the hegel macros produced no warnings
+   under any of them, which is worth a reassuring line in the reference (quickcheck, by
+   contrast, needs `#[allow(trivial_casts)]` on nearly every test in this crate).
+
+7. **The "Beware of properties that seem universal but aren't" guidance paid off directly**:
+   the affine round-trip is exactly such a property, and the SKILL.md instruction to
+   investigate before weakening (plus the porting.md warning about broadened generators
+   hitting documented precision limits) led to the commented-out upstream tests that settled
+   the real-bug-vs-unsound-property question. No change needed; this part of the skill is
+   load-bearing and accurate.
+
+8. **`one_of!` + `from_regex` composed cleanly** for the parse-robustness generator
+   (garbage text plus quantity-shaped strings). `.fullmatch(true)` worked as documented
+   with a non-trivial regex including `°F` (non-ASCII) alternatives.
